{"labeler": "2b794369-78c8-4e28-b3ba-eb0dd4a9ab2c", "timestamp": "2023-02-10T06:43:11.673254", "generation": 9, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Compute\n\\[\\frac{1}{2 \\sin 10^\\circ} - 2 \\sin 70^\\circ.\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "By product-to-sum,\n\\begin{align*}\n\\frac{1}{2 \\sin 10^\\circ} - 2 \\sin 70^\\circ &= \\frac{1 - 4 \\sin 10^\\circ \\sin 70^\\circ}{2 \\sin 10^\\circ} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1 - 2 (\\cos 60^\\circ - \\cos 80^\\circ)}{2 \\sin 10^\\circ} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 \\cos 80^\\circ}{2 \\sin 10^\\circ} = \\boxed{1}.\n\\end{align*}", "ground_truth_answer": "1", "pre_generated_steps": ["I see a difference of two fractions involving sine functions, so I wonder if there is a way to simplify them by using some trigonometric identities or transformations.", "One identity that comes to mind is the double angle formula for sine: $\\sin 2\\theta = 2 \\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta$.", "I can use this formula to rewrite $\\sin 70^\\circ$ as $\\sin 2 \\cdot 35^\\circ = 2 \\sin 35^\\circ \\cos 35^\\circ$.", "Then I can plug this into the second fraction and get $-2 \\sin 70^\\circ = -4 \\sin 35^\\circ \\cos 35^\\circ$.", "Now I have $\\frac{1}{2 \\sin 10^\\circ} - 4 \\sin 35^\\circ \\cos 35^\\circ$.", "I notice that both fractions have a factor of 2 in the denominator, so I can combine them under a common denominator of $4 \\sin 10^\\circ$.", "This gives me $\\frac{2 - 8 \\sin 10^\\circ \\sin 35^\\circ \\cos 35^\\circ}{4 \\sin 10^\\circ}$.", "I can simplify this by canceling out a factor of 2 from the numerator and the denominator, and get $\\frac{1 - 4 \\sin 10^\\circ \\sin 35^\\circ \\cos 35^\\circ}{2 \\sin 10^\\circ}$.", "Now I want to simplify the numerator further by using another trigonometric identity: the product-to-sum formula for sine: $\\sin A \\sin B = \\frac{1}{2} (\\cos (A - B) - \\cos (A + B))$.", "I can apply this formula to $\\sin 10^\\circ \\sin 35^\\circ$ and get $\\frac{1}{2} (\\cos (25^\\circ) - \\cos (45^\\circ))$.", "Then I can multiply this by $-4 \\cos 35^\\circ$ and get $-2 \\cos 25^\\circ \\cos 35^\\circ + 2 \\cos 45^\\circ \\cos 35^\\circ$.", "So the numerator becomes $1 - 2 \\cos 25^\\circ \\cos 35^\\circ + 2 \\cos 45^\\circ \\cos 35^\\circ$.", "I want to simplify this further by using another product-to-sum formula, this time for cosine: $\\cos A \\cos B = \\frac{1}{2} (\\cos (A - B) + \\cos (A + B))$.", "I can apply this formula to $\\cos 25^\\circ \\cos 35^\\circ$ and get $\\frac{1}{2} (\\cos (10^\\circ) + \\cos (60^\\circ))$.", "And I can apply this formula to $\\cos 45^\\circ \\cos 35^\\circ$ and get $\\frac{1}{2} (\\cos (10^\\circ) - \\cos (80^\\circ))$.", "So the numerator becomes $1 - \\cos (10^\\circ) - \\frac{1}{2} \\cos (60^\\circ) + \\cos (10^\\circ) - \\frac{1}{2} \\cos (80^\\circ)$.", "I can simplify this by canceling out the $\\cos (10^\\circ)$ terms and using the fact that $\\cos (60^\\circ) = \\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\cos (80^\\circ) = \\sin (10^\\circ)$.", "So the numerator becomes $1 - \\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{1}{2} \\sin (10^\\circ)$.", "Or, equivalently, $\\frac{3}{4} - \\frac{1}{2} \\sin (10^\\circ)$.", "So the final expression is $\\frac{\\frac{3}{4} - \\frac{1}{2} \\sin (10^\\circ)}{2 \\sin (10^\\circ)}$.", "I can simplify this by multiplying the numerator and the denominator by 4, and get $\\frac{3 - 2 \\sin (10^\\circ)}{8 \\sin (10^\\circ)}$.", "# Answer\n\n\\frac{3 - 2 \\sin (10^\\circ)}{8 \\sin (10^\\circ)}"], "pre_generated_answer": "\\frac{3 - 2 \\sin (10^\\circ)}{8 \\sin (10^\\circ)}", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.0034827440479279696}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "I see a difference of two fractions involving sine functions, so I wonder if there is a way to simplify them by using some trigonometric identities or transformations.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "One identity that comes to mind is the double 