{"labeler": "340d89bc-f5b7-45e9-b272-909ba68ee363", "timestamp": "2023-02-08T10:17:49.337399", "generation": 9, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "A right circular cylinder with its diameter equal to its height is inscribed in a right circular cone. The cone has diameter 10 and altitude 12, and the axes of the cylinder and cone coincide. Find the radius of the cylinder. Express your answer as a common fraction.", "ground_truth_solution": "Let the cylinder have radius $r$ and height $2r$. Since $\\triangle APQ$ is similar to $\\triangle AOB$, we have $$\\frac{12-2r}{r} = \\frac{12}{5}, \\text{ so } r = \\boxed{\\frac{30}{11}}.$$[asy]\ndraw((0,2)..(-6,0)--(6,0)..cycle);\ndraw((0,-2)..(-6,0)--(6,0)..cycle);\ndraw((0,1)..(-3,0)--(3,0)..cycle);\ndraw((0,-1)..(-3,0)--(3,0)..cycle);\nfill((-6,0.01)--(-6,-0.01)--(6,-0.01)--(6,0.01)--cycle,white);\ndraw((0,14)--(0,0)--(6,0),dashed);\ndraw((0,8)..(-3,7)--(3,7)..cycle);\ndraw((0,6)..(-3,7)--(3,7)..cycle);\nfill((-3,7.01)--(-3,6.99)--(3,6.99)--(3,7.01)--cycle,white);\ndraw((0,7)--(3,7),dashed);\ndraw((-6,0)--(0,14)--(6,0));\ndraw((-3,0)--(-3,7));\ndraw((3,0)--(3,7));\nlabel(\"{\\tiny O}\",(0,0),W);\nlabel(\"{\\tiny B}\",(6,0),E);\nlabel(\"{\\tiny P}\",(0,7),W);\nlabel(\"{\\tiny Q}\",(3,7),E);\nlabel(\"{\\tiny A}\",(0,14),E);\ndraw((0,-2.5)--(6,-2.5),Arrows);\ndraw((-6.5,0)--(-6.5,14),Arrows);\nlabel(\"{\\tiny 5}\",(3,-2.5),S);\nlabel(\"{\\tiny 12}\",(-6.5,7),W);\ndraw((10,0)--(15,0)--(10,12)--cycle);\ndraw((10,6)--(12.5,6));\ndraw((15.5,0)--(15.5,12),Arrows);\nlabel(\"{\\tiny O}\",(10,0),W);\nlabel(\"{\\tiny P}\",(10,6),W);\nlabel(\"{\\tiny A}\",(10,12),W);\nlabel(\"{\\tiny 2r}\",(10,3),W);\nlabel(\"{\\tiny 12-2r}\",(10,9),W);\nlabel(\"{\\tiny B}\",(15,0),E);\nlabel(\"{\\tiny Q}\",(12.5,6),E);\nlabel(\"{\\tiny 12}\",(15.5,6),E);\nlabel(\"{\\tiny 5}\",(12.5,0),S);\n[/asy]", "ground_truth_answer": "\\frac{30}{11}", "pre_generated_steps": ["I need to find the radius of the cylinder, which is also half of its diameter and height.", "I will draw a diagram to help me visualize the problem.", "I will label the radius of the cylinder as r, the radius of the cone as 5, and the height of the cone as 12.", "I will also draw a right triangle inside the cone, with one leg as the radius of the cone, the other leg as the height of the cone, and the hypotenuse as the slant height of the cone.", "I can use the Pythagorean theorem to find the slant height of the cone.", "It is sqrt(5^2 + 12^2) = sqrt(169) = 13.", "I will label the slant height as 13.", "I notice that the cylinder is tangent to the cone at two points, one at the base and one at the top.", "I will draw a line segment from the center of the cone's base to the point of tangency at the top.", "This segment is perpendicular to the top of the cylinder, so it forms a right angle with the radius of the cylinder.", "This means that the segment is also the height of the cylinder, which is 2r.", "I will label this segment as 2r.", "I see that this segment, the slant height, and the radius of the cone form another right triangle inside the cone.", "I can use similar triangles to find the value of r.", "The two right triangles are similar because they share an angle at the vertex of the cone.", "I will label this angle as theta.", "By similarity, I have the proportion 5 / 13 = r / (2r + 12).", "I will cross-multiply and simplify to get 10r + 60 = 13r.", "I will subtract 10r from both sides to get 3r = 60.", "I will divide both sides by 3 to get r = 20.", "This is the radius of the cylinder.\n\n# Answer\n\n20"], "pre_generated_answer": "20", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.003936370788538319}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "I need to find the radius of the cylinder, which is also half of its diameter and height.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I will draw a diagram to help me visualize the problem.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I will label the radius of the cylinder as r, the radius of the cone as 5, and the height of the cone as 12.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I will also draw a right triangle inside the cone, with one leg as the radius of the cone, the other leg as the height of the cone, and the hypotenuse as the slant height of the cone.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I can use the Pythagorean theorem to find the slant height of the cone.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "It is sqrt(5^2 + 12^2) = sqrt(169) = 13.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I will label the slant height as 13.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I notice that the cylinder is tangent to the cone at two points, one at the base and one at the top.", "rating": 0, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I will draw a line segment from the center of the cone's base to the point of tangency at the top.", "rating": 0, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "This segment is perpendicular to the top of the cylinder, so it forms a right angle with the radius of the cylinder.", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "This line segment is perpendicular to the cylinder's base and top, and it bisects them.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "This line segment is perpendicular to the cylinder's base and top, and it is also parallel to the cylinder's height.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "This segment is also the radius of the cylinder, so I will label it as r.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "This line segment is perpendicular to the tangent line, so it forms a right angle with the cylinder's axis.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 1409300, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "340d89bc-f5b7-45e9-b272-909ba68ee363", "timestamp": "2023-02-08T09:54:10.665086", "generation": 9, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Let $ABCDEF$ be a regular hexagon, and let $G,H,I$ be the midpoints of sides $AB,CD,EF$ respectively. If the area of $\\triangle GHI$ is $225$, what is the area of hexagon $ABCDEF$?", "ground_truth_solution": "We begin with a diagram of the given information: [asy]\nsize(4cm);\nreal x=sqrt(3);\npair d=(2,0); pair c=(1,x); pair b=(-1,x); pair a=-d; pair f=-c; pair e=-b;\npair g=(a+b)/2; pair h=(c+d)/2; pair i=(e+f)/2;\ndraw(a--b--c--d--e--f--a);\ndot(a); dot(b); dot(c); dot(d); dot(e); dot(f); dot(g); dot(h); dot(i);\ndraw(g--h--i--g);\nlabel(\"$A$\",a,W);\nlabel(\"$B$\",b,NNW);\nlabel(\"$C$\",c,NNE);\nlabel(\"$D$\",d,E);\nlabel(\"$E$\",e,SSE);\nlabel(\"$F$\",f,SSW);\nlabel(\"$G$\",g,WNW);\nlabel(\"$H$\",h,ENE);\nlabel(\"$I$\",i,S);\n[/asy]\n\nTo increase the symmetry in the diagram, we can draw in the long diagonals of $ABCDEF$ as well as the mirror image of $\\triangle GHI$ across these diagonals:\n\n[asy]\nsize(4cm);\nreal x=sqrt(3);\npair d=(2,0); pair c=(1,x); pair b=(-1,x); pair a=-d; pair f=-c; pair e=-b;\npair g=(a+b)/2; pair h=(c+d)/2; pair i=(e+f)/2;\nfill(g--h--i--cycle,gray);\ndraw(a--b--c--d--e--f--a);\ndot(a); dot(b); dot(c); dot(d); dot(e); dot(f); dot(g); dot(h); dot(i);\ndraw(g--h--i--g);\ndraw(a--d, dashed);\ndraw(b--e, dashed);\ndraw(c--f, dashed);\ndraw((-g)--(-h)--(-i)--(-g), dashed);\nlabel(\"$A$\",a,W);\nlabel(\"$B$\",b,NNW);\nlabel(\"$C$\",c,NNE);\nlabel(\"$D$\",d,E);\nlabel(\"$E$\",e,SSE);\nlabel(\"$F$\",f,SSW);\nlabel(\"$G$\",g,WNW);\nlabel(\"$H$\",h,ENE);\nlabel(\"$I$\",i,S);\n[/asy]\n\nThese additional lines divide $ABCDEF$ into $24$ congruent equilateral triangles, of which $\\triangle GHI$ covers exactly $9$. Thus each of the triangles has area $\\frac{225}{9}=25$, and hexagon $ABCDEF$ has area $24\\cdot 25=\\boxed{600}$.", "ground_truth_answer": "600", "pre_generated_steps": ["I notice that the triangle $GHI$ is formed by connecting the midpoints of every other side of the hexagon, so it is similar to the triangle formed by connecting the vertices of the same sides, which is $ACE$.", "Since the ratio of the sides of similar triangles is equal to the ratio of their areas, I can find the area of $\\triangle ACE$ by using the fact that $GHI$ is half the size of $ACE$ in each dimension.", "So the area of $\\triangle ACE$ is $225 \\times 2^2 = 900$.", "Now I notice that the hexagon is made up of six equilateral triangles, each with the same area as $\\triangle GHI$, so the area of the hexagon is $6 \\times 225 = 1350$.", "# Answer\n\n1350"], "pre_generated_answer": "1350", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.004240995338460953}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "I notice that the triangle $GHI$ is formed by connecting the midpoints of every other side of the hexagon, so it is similar to the triangle formed by connecting the vertices of the same sides, which is $ACE$.", "rating": 0, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Since the ratio of the sides of similar triangles is equal to the ratio of their areas, I can find the area of $\\triangle ACE$ by using the fact that $GHI$ is half the size of $ACE$ in each dimension.", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "In fact, since the ratio of the side lengths of $GHI$ and $ACE$ is $\\frac{1}{2}$, the ratio of their areas is $\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^2 = \\frac{1}{4}$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "The ratio of similarity between $GHI$ and $ACE$ is $1/2$, since the midpoints halve the sides of the hexagon.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "Since $G,H,I$ are midpoints, the ratio of similarity between $\\triangle GHI$ and $\\triangle ACE$ is $1:2$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "The ratio of similarity between $GHI$ and $ACE$ is $1:2$, since the midpoints halve the length of each side.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 2150755, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "2b794369-78c8-4e28-b3ba-eb0dd4a9ab2c", "timestamp": "2023-02-08T06:18:30.803342", "generation": 9, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "What is the value of $\\frac{2013^3-2 \\cdot 2013^2 \\cdot 2014+3 \\cdot 2013 \\cdot 2014^2-2014^3+1}{2013 \\cdot 2014}$?", "ground_truth_solution": "Let $a=2013$. The expression is equal to $\\frac{a^3-2a^2(a+1)+3a(a+1)^2-(a+1)^3+1}{a(a+1)}$. We notice a common factor of $a(a+1)$ in the second and third terms of the numerator, so we split the fraction apart: $$\\frac{-2a^2(a+1)+3a(a+1)^2}{a(a+1)}+\\frac{a^3-(a+1)^3+1}{a(a+1)}$$The first part of the expression is equal to $-2a+3(a+1)$, which simplifies to $a+3$.\n\nThe second part of the expression has a numerator that can be factored as a difference of cubes. We will concentrate on the numerator for now: \\begin{align*}\na^3-(a+1)^3+1 &= (a-(a+1))(a^2+a(a+1)+(a+1)^2)+1 \\\\\n&= (-1)(a^2+a^2+a+a^2+2a+1)+1 \\\\\n&= (-1)(3a^2+3a+1)+1 \\\\\n&= -3(a^2+a)-1+1 \\\\\n&= -3a(a+1)\n\\end{align*}Alternatively, we can expand the numerator, and factor:\n\\begin{align*}\na^3 - (a + 1)^3 + 1 &= a^3 - (a + 1)(a + 1)(a + 1) + 1 \\\\\n&= a^3 - (a^2 + 2a + 1)(a + 1) + 1 \\\\\n&= a^3 - (a^3 + 3a^2 + 3a + 1) + 1 \\\\\n&= a^3 - a^3 - 3a^2 - 3a - 1 + 1 \\\\\n&= -3a^2 - 3a \\\\\n&= -3(a^2 + a) \\\\\n&= -3a(a + 1).\n\\end{align*}Taken with the denominator, the second half of the expression simply equals $\\frac{-3a(a+1)}{a(a+1)}=-3$.\n\nPutting it back with the first half of the expression, the final expression equals $(a+3)+(-3)=a=\\boxed{2013}$.", "ground_truth_answer": "2013", "pre_generated_steps": ["This looks like a complicated fraction, but I notice that the numerator has a familiar pattern: it is a cubic polynomial in terms of 2013 and 2014.", "Specifically, it is $(2013-2014)^3+1$, which I can verify by expanding the cube and simplifying.\n[*,] So the fraction becomes $\\frac{(2013-2014)^3+1}{2013 \\cdot 2014}=\\frac{(-1)^3+1}{2013 \\cdot 2014}=\\frac{0}{2013 \\cdot 2014}=0$.", "# Answer\n\n0"], "pre_generated_answer": "0", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.003985644414295797}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "This looks like a complicated fraction, but I notice that the numerator has a familiar pattern: it is a cubic polynomial in terms of 2013 and 2014.", "rating": 0, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Specifically, it is $(2013-2014)^3+1$, which I can verify by expanding the cube and simplifying.\n[*,] So the fraction becomes $\\frac{(2013-2014)^3+1}{2013 \\cdot 2014}=\\frac{(-1)^3+1}{2013 \\cdot 2014}=\\frac{0}{2013 \\cdot 2014}=0$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "Specifically, it is $(2013-2014)^3+1$, which I can verify by expanding the expression using the binomial formula.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "In fact, it is exactly $(2013-2014)^3+1$, which can be verified by expanding the binomial and simplifying.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "In fact, it is the same as $(2013-2014)^3+1$, which I can see by expanding the cubic and simplifying the terms.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "Specifically, I recognize that the numerator is equal to $(2013-2014)^3+1$, which is just $(-1)^3+1=-1+1=0$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 33550, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "2b794369-78c8-4e28-b3ba-eb0dd4a9ab2c", "timestamp": "2023-02-08T06:09:53.961709", "generation": 9, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "If $0 \\le \\theta \\le 4 \\pi,$ find all values of $\\theta$ which satisfy\n\\[\\log_{\\frac{1}{2} \\sin 2 \\theta} \\sin \\theta = \\frac{1}{2}.\\]Enter all the solutions, separated by commas.", "ground_truth_solution": "From the given equation,\n\\[\\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\sin 2 \\theta \\right)^{\\frac{1}{2}} = \\sin \\theta.\\]Squaring both sides, we get\n\\[\\frac{1}{2} \\sin 2 \\theta = \\sin^2 \\theta.\\]Then $\\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta = \\sin^2 \\theta,$ so\n\\[\\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta - \\sin^2 \\theta = \\sin \\theta (\\sin \\theta - \\cos \\theta) = 0.\\]Thus, $\\sin \\theta = 0$ or $\\sin \\theta = \\cos \\theta.$\n\nIf $\\sin \\theta = 0,$ then $\\frac{1}{2} \\sin 2 \\theta = 0,$ which is not allowed as the base of a logarithm.\n\nOtherwise, $\\sin \\theta = \\cos \\theta.$  Then $\\tan \\theta = 1.$  The solutions to this equation are $\\frac{\\pi}{4},$ $\\frac{5 \\pi}{4},$ $\\frac{9 \\pi}{4},$ and $\\frac{13 \\pi}{4}.$  But $\\sin \\theta$ must be positive, in order to take the logarithm of it, so the only solutions are $\\boxed{\\frac{\\pi}{4}, \\frac{9 \\pi}{4}}.$", "ground_truth_answer": "\\frac{\\pi}{4}, \\frac{9 \\pi}{4}", "pre_generated_steps": ["This problem involves logarithms, trigonometry, and inequalities.", "I need to recall the definition of logarithms, some basic properties of logarithms, and some trigonometric identities.", "The definition of logarithms says that $\\log_b a = c$ if and only if $b^c = a.$", "So, I can rewrite the given equation as $\\left(\\frac{1}{2} \\sin 2 \\theta\\right)^{1/2} = \\sin \\theta.$", "This might make it easier to work with.", "Now, I need to recall some basic properties of logarithms, such as the fact that $\\log_b a = c$ implies that $a$ and $b$ must have the same sign and $b \\neq 1.$", 