{"labeler": "2249c427-dbda-4947-8b32-7eed7588e1d6", "timestamp": "2023-02-09T17:38:43.261277", "generation": 9, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Given that\n\\[2^{-\\frac{3}{2} + 2 \\cos \\theta} + 1 = 2^{\\frac{1}{4} + \\cos \\theta},\\]compute $\\cos 2 \\theta.$", "ground_truth_solution": "Let $x = 2^{\\cos \\theta}.$  Then the given equation becomes\n\\[2^{-\\frac{3}{2}} x^2 + 1 = 2^{\\frac{1}{4}} x.\\]We can re-write this as\n\\[2^{-\\frac{3}{2}} x^2 - 2^{\\frac{1}{4}} x + 1 = 0.\\]Since $2^{-\\frac{3}{2}} = (2^{-\\frac{3}{4}})^2$ and $2^{\\frac{1}{4}} = 2 \\cdot 2^{-\\frac{3}{4}},$ this quadratic factors as\n\\[(2^{-\\frac{3}{4}} x - 1)^2 = 0.\\]Then $2^{-\\frac{3}{4}} x = 1,$ so $x = 2^{\\frac{3}{4}}.$  Hence,\n\\[\\cos \\theta = \\frac{3}{4},\\]so $\\cos 2 \\theta = 2 \\cos^2 \\theta - 1 = 2 \\left( \\frac{3}{4} \\right)^2 - 1 = \\boxed{\\frac{1}{8}}.$", "ground_truth_answer": "\\frac{1}{8}", "pre_generated_steps": ["To solve this problem, I want to isolate the powers of 2 on one side of the equation and then compare the exponents.", "I can do this by subtracting 1 from both sides and then dividing by 2, which gives me\n\\[2^{-\\frac{3}{2} + 2 \\cos \\theta - 1} = 2^{\\frac{1}{4} + \\cos \\theta - 1}.\\]", "Now I can use the property that if two powers of the same base are equal, then their exponents must be equal as well.", "So I have\n\\[-\\frac{3}{2} + 2 \\cos \\theta - 1 = \\frac{1}{4**} + \\cos \\theta - 1.\\]", "Simplifying this equation, I get\n\\[2 \\cos \\theta - \\frac{5}{2} = \\cos \\theta - \\frac{3}{4}.\\]", "Subtracting $\\cos \\theta$ from both sides, I get\n\\[\\cos \\theta - \\frac{5}{2} = - \\frac{3}{4}.\\]", "Adding $\\frac{5}{2}$ to both sides, I get\n\\[\\cos \\theta = \\frac{5}{2} - \\frac{3}{4} = \\frac{7}{4}.\\]", "This is the value of $\\cos \\theta$, but the problem asks for $\\cos 2 \\theta$.", "To find that, I can use the double angle formula for cosine, which is\n\\[\\cos 2 \\theta = 2 \\cos^2 \\theta - 1.\\]", "Plugging in the value of $\\cos \\theta$ that I found, I get\n\\[\\cos 2 \\theta = 2 (\\frac{7}{4})^2 - 1 = \\frac{49}{8} - 1 = \\frac{41}{8}.\\]", "# Answer\n\n\\frac{41}{8}"], "pre_generated_answer": "\\frac{41}{8}", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.0036430538996808145}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "To solve this problem, I want to isolate the powers of 2 on one side of the equation and then compare the exponents.", "rating": 0, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I can do this by subtracting 1 from both sides and then dividing by 2, which gives me\n\\[2^{-\\frac{3}{2} + 2 \\cos \\theta - 1} = 2^{\\frac{1}{4} + \\cos \\theta - 1}.\\]", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Now I can use the property that if two powers of the same base are equal, then their exponents must be equal as well.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "So I have\n\\[-\\frac{3}{2} + 2 \\cos \\theta - 1 = \\frac{1}{4**} + \\cos \\theta - 1.\\]", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Simplifying this equation, I get\n\\[2 \\cos \\theta - \\frac{5}{2} = \\cos \\theta - \\frac{3}{4}.\\]", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Subtracting $\\cos \\theta$ from both sides, I get\n\\[\\cos \\theta - \\frac{5}{2} = - \\frac{3}{4}.\\]", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Adding $\\frac{5}{2}$ to both sides, I get\n\\[\\cos \\theta = \\frac{5}{2} - \\frac{3}{4} = \\frac{7}{4}.\\]", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "This is the value of $\\cos \\theta$, but the problem asks for $\\cos 2 \\theta$.", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "To find that, I can use the double angle formula for cosine, which is\n\\[\\cos 2 \\theta = 2 \\cos^2 \\theta - 1.\\]", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Plugging in the value of $\\cos \\theta$ that I found, I get\n\\[\\cos 2 \\theta = 2 (\\frac{7}{4})^2 - 1 = \\frac{49}{8} - 1 = \\frac{41}{8}.\\]", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "# Answer\n\n\\frac{41}{8}", "rating": null, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}], "total_time": 356193, "finish_reason": "give_up"}}
{"labeler": "2b794369-78c8-4e28-b3ba-eb0dd4a9ab2c", "timestamp": "2023-02-06T04:00:34.418432", "generation": 9, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "What is the value of the sum $S=\\sum_{k=0}^{49}(-1)^k\\binom{99}{2k}=\\binom{99}{0}-\\binom{99}{2}+\\binom{99}{4}-\\cdots -\\binom{99}{98}?$\n(A) $-2^{50}$ (B) $-2^{49}$ (C) 0 (D) $2^{49}$ (E) $2^{50}$", "ground_truth_solution": "By the Binomial Theorem, $(1+i)^{99}=\\sum_{n=0}^{99}\\binom{99}{j}i^n =$ $\\binom{99}{0}i^0+\\binom{99}{1}i^1+\\binom{99}{2}i^2+\\binom{99}{3}i^3+\\binom{99}{4}i^4+\\cdots +\\binom{99}{98}i^{98}$.\nUsing the fact that $i^1=i$, $i^2=-1$, $i^3=-i$, $i^4=1$, and $i^{n+4}=i^n$, the sum becomes:\n$(1+i)^{99}=\\binom{99}{0}+\\binom{99}{1}i-\\binom{99}{2}-\\binom{99}{3}i+\\binom{99}{4}+\\cdots -\\binom{99}{98}$.\nSo, $Re[(1+i)^{99}]=\\binom{99}{0}-\\binom{99}{2}+\\binom{99}{4}-\\cdots -\\binom{99}{98} = S$.\nUsing De Moivre's Theorem, $(1+i)^{99}=[\\sqrt{2}cis(45^\\circ)]^{99}=\\sqrt{2^{99}}\\cdot cis(99\\cdot45^\\circ)=2^{49}\\sqrt{2}\\cdot cis(135^\\circ) = -2^{49}+2^{49}i$.\nAnd finally, $S=Re[-2^{49}+2^{49}i] = \\boxed{-2^{49}}$.", "ground_truth_answer": "-2^{49}", "pre_generated_steps": ["I recognize that this sum is related to the binomial expansion of $(1+i)^{99}$, where $i$ is the imaginary unit.", "To see why, I recall that $(1+i)^{99}=\\sum_{k=0}^{99}\\binom{99}{k}i^k$.", "If I group the terms by the parity of $k$, I get $(1+i)^{99}=\\sum_{k=0}^{49}\\binom{99}{2k}i^{2k}+\\sum_{k=0}^{49}\\binom{99}{2k+1}i^{2k+1}$.", "Since $i^2=-1$ and $i^3=-i$, I can simplify this expression to $(1+i)^{99}=\\sum_{k=0}^{49}\\binom{99}{2k}(-1)^k+\\sum_{k=0}^{49}\\binom{99}{2k+1}(-1)^k i$.", "Comparing this with the sum $S$, I see that $S$ is the real part of $(1+i)^{99}$.", "To find the real part of $(1+i)^{99}$, I can write $(1+i)^{99}$ in polar form, using the fact that $1+i=\\sqrt{2}e^{i\\pi/4}$.", "Then $(1+i)^{99}=(\\sqrt{2}e^{i\\pi/4})^{99}=\\sqrt{2}^{99}e^{i99\\pi/4}$.", "The real part of this expression is $\\sqrt{2}^{99}\\cos(99\\pi/4)$.", "To simplify this, I use the periodicity of the cosine function and the fact that $\\cos(\\pi/4)=\\sqrt{2}/2$.", "I get $\\cos(99\\pi/4)=\\cos(24\\pi+\\pi/4)=\\cos(\\pi/4)=\\sqrt{2}/2$.", "Therefore, the real part of $(1+i)^{99}$ is $\\sqrt{2}^{99}\\sqrt{2}/2=\\sqrt{2}^{100}/2=2^{50}/2=2^{49}$.", "This means that $S=2^{49}$, which is option (D).", "# Answer\n\n2^{49}"], "pre_generated_answer": "2^{49}", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.004964458319260991}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "I recognize that this sum is related to the binomial expansion of $(1+i)^{99}$, where $i$ is the imaginary unit.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "To see why, I recall that $(1+i)^{99}=\\sum_{k=0}^{99}\\binom{99}{k}i^k$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "If I group the terms by the parity of $k$, I get $(1+i)^{99}=\\sum_{k=0}^{49}\\binom{99}{2k}i^{2k}+\\sum_{k=0}^{49}\\binom{99}{2k+1}i^{2k+1}$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Since $i^2=-1$ and $i^3=-i$, I can simplify this expression to $(1+i)^{99}=\\sum_{k=0}^{49}\\binom{99}{2k}(-1)^k+\\sum_{k=0}^{49}\\binom{99}{2k+1}(-1)^k i$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Comparing this with the sum $S$, I see that $S$ is the real part of $(1+i)^{99}$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "To find the real part of $(1+i)^{99}$, I can write $(1+i)^{99}$ in polar form, using the fact that $1+i=\\sqrt{2}e^{i\\pi/4}$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Then $(1+i)^{99}=(\\sqrt{2}e^{i\\pi/4})^{99}=\\sqrt{2}^{99}e^{i99\\pi/4}$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "The real part of this expression is $\\sqrt{2}^{99}\\cos(99\\pi/4)$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "To simplify this, I use the periodicity of the cosine function and the fact that $\\cos(\\pi/4)=\\sqrt{2}/2$.", "rating": 0, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I get $\\cos(99\\pi/4)=\\cos(24\\pi+\\pi/4)=\\cos(\\pi/4)=\\sqrt{2}/2$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "I get that $\\cos(99\\pi/4)=\\cos(24\\pi+\\pi/4)=\\cos(\\pi/4)=\\sqrt{2}/2$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I get $\\sqrt{2}^{99}\\cos(99\\pi/4)=\\sqrt{2}^{99}\\cos(3\\pi/4)=-\\sqrt{2}^{98}$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I get $\\cos(99\\pi/4)=\\cos(2\\pi+27\\pi/4)=\\cos(27\\pi/4)=\\sqrt{2}/2$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 144653, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "340d89bc-f5b7-45e9-b272-909ba68ee363", "timestamp": "2023-02-06T03:38:02.558200", "generation": 9, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "In triangle $ABC$, $A'$, $B'$, and $C'$ are on the sides $BC$, $AC$, and $AB$, respectively. Given that $AA'$, $BB'$, and $CC'$ are concurrent at the point $O$, and that $\\frac{AO}{OA'}+\\frac{BO}{OB'}+\\frac{CO}{OC'}=92$, find $\\frac{AO}{OA'}\\cdot \\frac{BO}{OB'}\\cdot \\frac{CO}{OC'}$.", "ground_truth_solution": "Let $K_A=[BOC], K_B=[COA],$ and $K_C=[AOB].$ Due to triangles $BOC$ and $ABC$ having the same base,\\[\\frac{AO}{OA'}+1=\\frac{AA'}{OA'}=\\frac{[ABC]}{[BOC]}=\\frac{K_A+K_B+K_C}{K_A}.\\]Therefore, we have\\[\\frac{AO}{OA'}=\\frac{K_B+K_C}{K_A}\\]\\[\\frac{BO}{OB'}=\\frac{K_A+K_C}{K_B}\\]\\[\\frac{CO}{OC'}=\\frac{K_A+K_B}{K_C}.\\]Thus, we are given\\[\\frac{K_B+K_C}{K_A}+\\frac{K_A+K_C}{K_B}+\\frac{K_A+K_B}{K_C}=92.\\]Combining and expanding gives\\[\\frac{K_A^2K_B+K_AK_B^2+K_A^2K_C+K_AK_C^2+K_B^2K_C+K_BK_C^2}{K_AK_BK_C}=92.\\]We desire $\\frac{(K_B+K_C)(K_C+K_A)(K_A+K_B)}{K_AK_BK_C}.$ Expanding this gives\\[\\frac{K_A^2K_B+K_AK_B^2+K_A^2K_C+K_AK_C^2+K_B^2K_C+K_BK_C^2}{K_AK_BK_C}+2=\\boxed{94}.\\]", "ground_truth_answer": "94", "pre_generated_steps": ["This problem looks like it involves some kind of ratio or similarity theorem.", "I recall that there is a theorem that says that if $AA'$, $BB'$, and $CC'$ are concurrent cevians (segments from a vertex to the opposite side) in a triangle, then $\\frac{BA'}{A'C}\\cdot \\frac{CB'}{B'A}\\cdot \\frac{AC'}{C'B}=1$.", "This is sometimes called Ceva's theorem, and it can be proved using areas of triangles.", "I wonder if I can use this theorem to relate the given expression to the one I want to find.", "I notice that $\\frac{BA'}{A'C}=\\frac{AO+OA'}{OA'}-1=\\frac{AO}{OA'}$.", "Similarly, $\\frac{CB'}{B'A}=\\frac{BO}{OB'}$ and $\\frac{AC'}{C'B}=\\frac{CO}{OC'}$.", "So, Ceva's theorem tells me that $\\frac{AO}{OA'}\\cdot \\frac{BO}{OB'}\\cdot \\frac{CO}{OC'}=1$.", "This is exactly what I want to find, so I don't need to use the given expression at all.\n\n# Answer\n\n1"], "pre_generated_answer": "1", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.004730913016278203}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "This problem looks like it involves some kind of ratio or similarity theorem.", "rating": 0, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I recall that there is a theorem that says that if $AA'$, $BB'$, and $CC'$ are concurrent cevians (segments from a vertex to the opposite side) in a triangle, then $\\frac{BA'}{A'C}\\cdot \\frac{CB'}{B'A}\\cdot \\frac{AC'}{C'B}=1$.", "rating": 0, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "This is sometimes called Ceva's theorem, and it can be proved using areas of triangles.", "rating": 0, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I wonder if I can use this theorem to relate the given expression to the one I want to find.", "rating": 0, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I notice that $\\frac{BA'}{A'C}=\\frac{AO+OA'}{OA'}-1=\\frac{AO}{OA'}$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "I notice that $\\frac{AO}{OA'}$ is the same as $\\frac{AO}{OA'}\\cdot \\frac{BA'}{BA'}$, because multiplying by 1 does not change anything.", "rating": 0, "flagged": false}, {"text": "Let me try to write $\\frac{AO}{OA'}$ in terms of the other ratios.", "rating": 0, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I notice that $\\frac{AO}{OA'}$ is the same as $\\frac{AO}{OA'}\\cdot \\frac{AC}{AC}$ by multiplying and dividing by the same length.", "rating": 0, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I notice that the given expression has the lengths of the cevians in the numerator and the lengths of the remaining parts of the sides in the denominator, so maybe I can flip them and get something useful.", "rating": 0, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 548184, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "340d89bc-f5b7-45e9-b272-909ba68ee363", "timestamp": "2023-02-06T03:06:52.599621", "generation": 9, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Let $\\star (x)$ be the sum of the digits of a positive integer $x$. $\\mathcal{S}$ is the set of positive integers such that for all elements $n$ in $\\mathcal{S}$, we have that $\\star (n)=12$ and $0\\le n< 10^{7}$. If $m$ is the number of elements in $\\mathcal{S}$, compute $\\star(m)$.", "ground_truth_solution": "Equivalently, we need to place 12 indistinguishable balls into 7 distinguishable boxes so that no box contains more than 9 balls. There are ${12 + 7 - 1 \\choose 7 - 1} = {18 \\choose 6} = 18,564$ ways to place 12 objects into 7 boxes. Of these, 7 place all 12 into a single box. $7 \\cdot 6 = 42$ place 11 in one box and 1 in a second. $7 \\cdot 6 = 42$ place 10 into one box and 2 into a second. $7 \\cdot \\frac{6\\cdot 5}{2} = 105$ place 10 into one box and 1 into each of two others. Thus, this gives us $m = 18564 - 7 - 42 - 42 - 105 = 18368$ so $\\star(m) = 1 + 8 + 3 + 6 + 8 = \\boxed{26}$.", "ground_truth_answer": "26", "pre_generated_steps": ["I need to find the number of positive integers less than $10^{7}$ that have a digit sum of $12$.", "One way to approach this problem is to use a generating function that counts the number of ways to get a digit sum of $12$ using seven or fewer digits from $0$ to $9$.", "I can represent each digit as a term of the form $1+x+x^2+\\cdots +x^9$, since each digit can contribute from $0$ to $9$ to the digit sum.", "The generating function for the digit sum of a seven-digit number is then the product of seven such terms: $(1+x+\\cdots +x^9)^7$.", "To find the number of ways to get a digit sum of $12$, I need to find the coefficient of $x^{12}$ in this generating function.", "I can use the binomial theorem to expand each term as $\\sum_{k=0}^{9}\\binom{9}{k}x^k$, and then multiply the seven sums using the multinomial theorem.", "Alternatively, I can use a combinatorial argument to count the number of ways to distribute $12$ units among seven bins, each with a capacity of $9$.", "This is equivalent to finding the number of non-negative integer solutions to the equation $x_1+x_2+\\cdots +x_7=12$, subject to the condition that $x_i\\leq 9$ for each $i$.", "I can use the stars and bars method to count the number of solutions without the capacity constraint, which is $\\binom{12+7-1}{7-1}=\\binom{18}{6}=18564$.", "Then I need to subtract the number of solutions that violate the capacity constraint, which means that one of the bins has $10$ or more units.", "There can be only one such bin, since $10+10>12$.", "I can choose that bin in $7$ ways, and then distribute the remaining $2$ units among the other six bins in $\\binom{2+6-1}{6-1}=\\binom{7}{5}=21$ ways.", "So the number of solutions that violate the capacity constraint is $7\\times 21=147$.", "Therefore, the number of solutions that satisfy the capacity constraint, and hence the number of seven-digit numbers with a digit sum of $12$, is $18564-147=18417$.", "This means that $m=18417$, and to find $\\star(m)$, I just need to add the digits of $m$.", "I see that $m=18417=10000+8000+400+10+7$, so $\\star(m)=1+8+4+1+7=21$.", "# Answer\n\n21"], "pre_generated_answer": "21", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.004753798090013165}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "I need to find the number of positive integers less than $10^{7}$ that have a digit sum of $12$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "One way to approach this problem is to use a generating function that counts the number of ways to get a digit sum of $12$ using seven or fewer digits from $0$ to $9$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "I can think of each such integer as a string of seven digits, some of which may be zeros.", "rating": 0, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I could try to list them all, but that would take too long.", "rating": 0, "flagged": false}, {"text": "One way to do this is to use the stars and bars method, where I imagine $12$ stars representing the units of digit sum, and $6$ bars separating the digits of the number.", "rating": 0, "flagged": false}, {"text": "One way to approach this problem is to consider how many ways I can distribute the value of $12$ among the seven digits of the number.", "rating": 0, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 700260, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "340d89bc-f5b7-45e9-b272-909ba68ee363", "timestamp": "2023-02-06T02:55:02.330172", "generation": 9, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "What is the largest four-digit negative integer congruent to $1 \\pmod{23}?$", "ground_truth_solution": "An integer that is congruent to $1 \\pmod{23}$ is of the form $23n+1$.\n\nTherefore, we form the inequality $23n+1<-999$, and find the largest possible integer $n$. We get \\begin{align*}\n23n+1&<-999 \\\\\n23n&<-1000\\\\\nn&<-\\frac{1000}{23} \\approx -43.48.\n\\end{align*} The largest possible negative integer $n$ is $-44$. We plug it in for $n$ to get $23 \\cdot -44 +1 =\\boxed{-1011}$.", "ground_truth_answer": "-1011", "pre_generated_steps": ["To find the largest four-digit negative integer congruent to $1 \\pmod{23}$, I need to find the smallest positive integer $k$ such that $23k + 1$ is a four-digit number.", "If $23k + 1$ is a four-digit number, then $1000 \\leq 23k + 1 \\leq 9999$.", "Subtracting $1$ from both sides, I get $999 \\leq 23k \\leq 9998$.", "Dividing both sides by $23$, I get $\\frac{999}{23} \\leq k \\leq \\frac{9998}{23}$.", "Using a calculator, I can approximate these fractions as $43.43 \\leq k \\leq 434.7$.", "Since $k$ has to be a positive integer, I can round up the lower bound and round down the upper bound to get $44 \\leq k \\leq 434$.", "The largest possible value of $k$ is $434$, so the largest four-digit negative integer congruent to $1 \\pmod{23}$ is $-23 \\cdot 434 + 1 = -9981$.\n\n# Answer\n\n-9981"], "pre_generated_answer": "-9981", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.0047544240054253565}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "To find the largest four-digit negative integer congruent to $1 \\pmod{23}$, I need to find the smallest positive integer $k$ such that $23k + 1$ is a four-digit number.", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "To find an integer that is congruent to $1 \\pmod{23}$, I need to add or subtract multiples of $23$ from $1$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "To find the largest negative integer congruent to $1 \\pmod{23}$, I need to subtract multiples of 23 from 1 until I get a number that is less than -10000.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I know that two integers are congruent modulo $n$ if they have the same remainder when divided by $n$.", "rating": 0, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I want to find an integer that is equivalent to 1 modulo 23, which means it leaves a remainder of 1 when divided by 23.", "rating": 1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 232899, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "340d89bc-f5b7-45e9-b272-909ba68ee363", "timestamp": "2023-02-06T01:55:26.648107", "generation": 9, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "A scalene triangle has side lengths which are prime numbers and the  length of its perimeter is also prime. What is its smallest possible  perimeter?", "ground_truth_solution": "The first few prime numbers are: $2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17,\\ldots$.  Since the triangle is scalene, all the sides are different primes.\n\nIf one side is 2, then the other two sides must be odd.  Then the perimeter of the triangle would be even.  But the perimeter must also be greater than 2, so it cannot be prime.  This means that none of the sides can be 2.\n\nNow, suppose one side is 3.  Let the other two sides be $p$ and $q,$ where $p < q.$  Since all the sides are different,\n\\[3 < p < q.\\]Also, by the Triangle Inequality, $p + 3 > q,$ so\n\\[p > q - 3.\\]Since $p < q,$ the only possible values of $p$ are $q - 2$ and $q - 1.$\n\nSince $p$ is a prime greater than 3, $p$ is odd.  If $p = q - 1,$ then $q = p + 1$ is even, which means $q$ is not prime.  Therefore, $p = q - 2,$ or\n\\[q = p + 2.\\]As a number, $p$ must be of the form $3k,$ $3k + 1,$ or $3k + 2.$  Since $p$ is prime, $p$ cannot be of the form $3k.$  If $p = 3k + 1,$ then $q = p + 2 = 3k + 3 = 3(k + 1),$ which is not prime.  Therefore, $p = 3k + 2.$  Then $q = p + 2 = 3k + 4,$ and the perimeter of the triangle is\n\\[p + q + 3 = (3k + 2) + (3k + 4) + 3 = 6k + 9 = 3(2k + 3).\\]Since this is divisible by 3, the perimeter cannot be prime.  This tells us that none of the sides can be equal to 3 either.\n\nNote that $5 + 7 + 11 = 23$ is prime, so the smallest possible perimeter is $\\boxed{23}.$", "ground_truth_answer": "23", "pre_generated_steps": ["A scalene triangle is one where all three sides have different lengths.", "Since the side lengths are prime numbers, they must be odd, except for one possible side of length 2.", "But if one side is 2, then the other two sides must be odd and their sum must be odd as well. This would violate the triangle inequality, which says that the sum of any two sides of a triangle must be greater than the third side.", "So all three sides must be odd primes.", "The smallest odd primes are 3, 5, and 7.", "If I try to make a triangle with these three numbers, I see that 3 + 5 = 8, which is not greater than 7, so this also violates the triangle inequality.", "The next smallest odd prime is 11.", "If I try to make a triangle with 3, 5, and 11, I see that 3 + 5 = 8, which is less than 11, so this still violates the triangle inequality.", "The next smallest odd prime is 13.", "If I try to make a triangle with 3, 5, and 13, I see that 3 + 5 = 8, which is less than 13, so this still violates the triangle inequality.", "The next smallest odd prime is 17.", "If I try to make a triangle with 3, 5, and 17, I see that 3 + 5 = 8, which is less than 17, so this still violates the triangle inequality.", "The next smallest odd prime is 19.", "If I try to make a triangle with 3, 5, and 19, I see that 3 + 5 = 8, which is less than 19, so this still violates the triangle inequality.", "The next smallest odd prime is 23.", "If I try to make a triangle with 3, 5, and 23, I see that 3 + 5 = 8, which is less than 23, so this still violates the triangle inequality.", "I notice that the problem is that 3 and 5 are too small compared to the other primes, and their sum is always 8, which is too small to make a triangle with any other prime.", "So I need to try larger primes for the two smaller sides.", "The next smallest odd prime after 5 is 7.", "If I try to make a triangle with 7, 11, and 13, I see that 7 + 11 = 18, which is greater than 13, and 7 + 13 = 20, which is greater than 11, and 11 + 13 = 24, which is greater than 7, so this satisfies the triangle inequality.", "The perimeter of this triangle is 7 + 11 + 13 = 31, which is also prime.", "So this is the smallest possible perimeter for a scalene triangle with prime side lengths and a prime perimeter.\n\n# Answer\n\n31"], "pre_generated_answer": "31", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.004801967247194123}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "A scalene triangle is one where all three sides have different lengths.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Since the side lengths are prime numbers, they must be odd, except for one possible side of length 2.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "But if one side is 2, then the other two sides must be odd and their sum must be odd as well. This would violate the triangle inequality, which says that the sum of any two sides of a triangle must be greater than the third side.", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "But if one side is 2, then the other two sides must be equal, or else the triangle inequality would be violated. For example, 2, 3, 5 is not a valid triangle, because 2 + 3 < 5.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "However, if one side is 2, then the other two sides must have the same parity (odd or even) to satisfy the triangle inequality.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "If the perimeter is also prime, it must be odd, since the sum of two odd numbers and an even number is odd.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "The sum of three odd numbers is always odd, and the sum of two odd numbers and an even number is also odd, so the perimeter must be an odd prime number.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 9445327, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "340d89bc-f5b7-45e9-b272-909ba68ee363", "timestamp": "2023-02-05T10:18:21.301633", "generation": 9, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Find the sum of the rational roots of $g(x)=x^3-9x^2+16x-4$.", "ground_truth_solution": "By the Rational Root Theorem, any rational root $p/q$ of $g(x)$ must have $p$ divide $4$ and $q$ divide 1. In particular, this means any rational root must be an integer divisor of 4.\n\nBy trying out the integer factors of 4, we find that $g(2) = 8-9\\cdot4+16\\cdot2-4=0$. Hence by the Factor theorem, $x-2$ is a factor of $g(x)$. With polynomial division, we can write $g(x) = (x-2)(x^2-7x+2).$ We can find the remaining roots of $g(x)$ by finding the roots of $x^2-7x+2$ using the quadratic formula. This gives us\n\\[x = \\frac{7 \\pm \\sqrt{49-8} }{2} =\\frac{7 \\pm \\sqrt{41} }{2} .\\]Since these are definitely not rational, the sum of the rational roots of $g(x)$ is $\\boxed{2}.$", "ground_truth_answer": "2", "pre_generated_steps": ["To find the rational roots of $g(x)$, I can use the rational root theorem, which says that any rational root of the form $p/q$, where $p$ and $q$ are integers, must have $p$ as a factor of the constant term and $q$ as a factor of the leading coefficient.", "The constant term is $-4$, so its possible factors are $\\pm 1, \\pm 2, \\pm 4$.", "The leading coefficient is $1$, so its only factor is $\\pm 1$.", "This means that the possible rational roots are $\\pm 1, \\pm 2, \\pm 4$.", "I can check each of these values by plugging them into $g(x)$ and seeing if I get zero.", "If I try $x=1$, I get $g(1)=1-9+16-4=4$, which is not zero.", "If I try $x=-1$, I get $g(-1)=-1-9-16-4=-30$, which is not zero.", "If I try $x=2$, I get $g(2)=8-36+32-4=0$, which is zero.", "If I try $x=-2$, I get $g(-2)=-8-36-32-4=-80$, which is not zero.", "If I try $x=4$, I get $g(4)=64-144+64-4=-20$, which is not zero.", "If I try $x=-4$, I get $g(-4)=-64-144-64-4=-276$, which is not zero.", "So the only rational root of $g(x)$ is $x=2$.", "The sum of the rational roots of $g(x)$ is therefore 2.", "# Answer\n\n2"], "pre_generated_answer": "2", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.0048141222231357405}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "To find the rational roots of $g(x)$, I can use the rational root theorem, which says that any rational root of the form $p/q$, where $p$ and $q$ are integers, must have $p$ as a factor of the constant term and $q$ as a factor of the leading coefficient.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "The constant term is $-4$, so its possible factors are $\\pm 1, \\pm 2, \\pm 4$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "The leading coefficient is $1$, so its only factor is $\\pm 1$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "This means that the possible rational roots are $\\pm 1, \\pm 2, \\pm 4$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I can check each of these values by plugging them into $g(x)$ and seeing if I get zero.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "If I try $x=1$, I get $g(1)=1-9+16-4=4$, which is not zero.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "If I try $x=-1$, I get $g(-1)=-1-9-16-4=-30$, which is not zero.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "If I try $x=2$, I get $g(2)=8-36+32-4=0$, which is zero.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "If I try $x=-2$, I get $g(-2)=-8-36-32-4=-80$, which is not zero.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "If I try $x=4$, I get $g(4)=64-144+64-4=-20$, which is not zero.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "If I try $x=-4$, I get $g(-4)=-64-144-64-4=-276$, which is not zero.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "So the only rational root of $g(x)$ is $x=2$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "The sum of the rational roots of $g(x)$ is therefore 2.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "# Answer\n\n2", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}], "total_time": 365339, "finish_reason": "solution"}}
{"labeler": "340d89bc-f5b7-45e9-b272-909ba68ee363", "timestamp": "2023-02-05T10:12:06.793554", "generation": 9, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "An arithmetic sequence with first term $1$ has a common difference of $6$. A second sequence begins with $4$ and has a common difference of $7$. In the range of $1$ to $100$, what is the largest number common to both sequences?", "ground_truth_solution": "Let $a$ be the smallest common term. We know that \\begin{align*}\na & \\equiv 1 \\pmod 6\\\\\na & \\equiv 4 \\pmod 7\n\\end{align*} We see that $a \\equiv 1 \\pmod 6$ means that there exists a non-negative integer $n$ such that $a=1+6n$. Substituting this into $a \\equiv 4 \\pmod 7$ yields \\[1+6n\\equiv 4\\pmod 7\\implies n\\equiv 4\\pmod 7\\] So $n$ has a lower bound of $4$. Then $n\\ge 4\\implies a=1+6n\\ge 25$. We see that $25$ satisfies both congruences so $a=25$. If $b$ is any common term, subtracting $25$ from both sides of both congruences gives \\begin{align*}\nb-25 & \\equiv -24\\equiv 0\\pmod 6 \\\\\nb-25 & \\equiv -21\\equiv 0\\pmod 7\n\\end{align*} Since $\\gcd(6,7)=1$, we have $b-25\\equiv 0\\pmod {6\\cdot 7}$, that is, $b\\equiv 25\\pmod{42}.$ So $b=25+42m$ for some integer $m$. The largest such number less than $100$ is $\\boxed{67}$, which happens to satisfies the original congruences.", "ground_truth_answer": "67", "pre_generated_steps": ["I want to find a number that is both of the form $1 + 6n$ and $4 + 7m$, where $n$ and $m$ are positive integers.", "I can rewrite this as $6n - 7m = 3$.", "This is a linear diophantine equation, and I can use the Euclidean algorithm to find a solution.", "I start by dividing $6$ by $7$ and getting a quotient of $0$ and a remainder of $6$.", "Then I divide $7$ by $6$ and get a quotient of $1$ and a remainder of $1$.", "This means that $7 - 1 \\cdot 6 = 1$, which is a solution to $6x - 7y = 1$.", "To get a solution to $6n - 7m = 3$, I can multiply both sides by $3$ and get $3(7 - 1 \\cdot 6) = 3 \\cdot 1$.", "This simplifies to $21 - 18 = 3$, so $n = -3$ and $m = -3$ is a solution.", "But I want $n$ and $m$ to be positive, so I need to find another solution that is equivalent to this one.", "I can use the fact that if $(n, m)$ is a solution, then so is $(n + 7k, m + 6k)$ for any integer $k$.", "I try $k = 1$ and get $n = 4$ and $m = 3$.", "This means that $1 + 6 \\cdot 4 = 25$ and $4 + 7 \\cdot 3 = 25$ are both terms in the sequences.", "This is a possible candidate for the largest common number, but I need to check if there is a larger one.", "I try $k = 2$ and get $n = 11$ and $m = 9$.", "This means that $1 + 6 \\cdot 11 = 67$ and $4 + 7 \\cdot 9 = 67$ are also common terms.", "This is larger than $25$, so I update my candidate.", "I try $k = 3$ and get $n = 18$ and $m = 15$.", "This means that $1 + 6 \\cdot 18 = 109$ and $4 + 7 \\cdot 15 = 109$ are also common terms.", "But this is larger than $100$, so I cannot use it.", "I conclude that the largest number common to both sequences in the range of $1$ to $100$ is $67$.", "# Answer\n\n67"], "pre_generated_answer": "67", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.004823913879264891}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "I want to find a number that is both of the form $1 + 6n$ and $4 + 7m$, where $n$ and $m$ are positive integers.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I can rewrite this as $6n - 7m = 3$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "This is a linear diophantine equation, and I can use the Euclidean algorithm to find a solution.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "I start by dividing $6$ by $7$ and getting a quotient of $0$ and a remainder of $6$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "Then I divide $7$ by $6$ and get a quotient of $1$ and a remainder of $1$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "This means that $7 - 1 \\cdot 6 = 1$, which is a solution to $6x - 7y = 1$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "To get a solution to $6n - 7m = 3$, I can multiply both sides by $3$ and get $3(7 - 1 \\cdot 6) = 3 \\cdot 1$.", "rating": 1, "flagged": null}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": 0}, {"completions": [{"text": "This simplifies to $21 - 18 = 3$, so $n = -3$ and $m = -3$ is a solution.", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "This implies that $n = -3$ and $m = -9$ is a solution.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "Simplifying, I get $21 - 18 = 3$.", "rating": 0, "flagged": false}, {"text": "This gives me $21 - 18 = 3$, so $n = -3$ and $m = -3$ is a solution.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}, {"text": "Simplifying, this gives me $21 - 18 = 3$, or $6 \\cdot (-3) - 7 \\cdot (-2) = 3$.", "rating": -1, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 441740, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "340d89bc-f5b7-45e9-b272-909ba68ee363", "timestamp": "2023-02-05T07:49:24.678538", "generation": 9, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "Find the equation of the plane which bisects the angle between the planes $3x - 6y + 2z + 5 = 0$ and $4x - 12y + 3z - 3 = 0,$ and which contains the point $(-5,-1,-5).$  Enter your answer in the form\n\\[Ax + By + Cz + D = 0,\\]where $A,$ $B,$ $C,$ $D$ are integers such that $A > 0$ and $\\gcd(|A|,|B|,|C|,|D|) = 1.$", "ground_truth_solution": "Suppose $P = (x,y,z)$ is a point that lies on a plane that bisects the angle between the planes $3x - 6y + 2z + 5 = 0$ and $4x - 12y + 3z - 3 = 0.$  (Note that there are two such bisecting planes.)  Then the distance from $P$ to both planes must be equal, so\n\\[\\frac{|3x - 6y + 2z + 5|}{\\sqrt{3^2 + (-6)^2 + 2^2}} = \\frac{|4x - 12y + 3z - 3|}{\\sqrt{4^2 + (-12)^2 + 3^2}}.\\]Then\n\\[\\frac{|3x - 6y + 2z + 5|}{7} = \\frac{|4x - 12y + 3z - 3|}{13}.\\]We want to remove the absolute value signs, in order to obtain the equation of a plane.  Checking the sign of both sides when $(x,y,z) = (-5,-1,-5)$ leads us to\n\\[\\frac{3x - 6y + 2z + 5}{7} = \\frac{4x - 12y + 3z - 3}{13}.\\]This simplifies to $\\boxed{11x + 6y + 5z + 86 = 0}.$", "ground_truth_answer": "11x + 6y + 5z + 86 = 0", "pre_generated_steps": ["I know that the angle bisector plane must be parallel to the cross product of the normals of the given planes, since that vector is perpendicular to both planes and thus lies on the angle bisector.", "So I need to find the cross product of $(3,-6,2)$ and $(4,-12,3)$ using the determinant formula.", "That gives me $\\begin{vmatrix} \\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 3 & -6 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -12 & 3 \\end{vmatrix} = (-12+24)\\mathbf{i} - (9-8)\\mathbf{j} + (-36+24)\\mathbf{k} = 12\\mathbf{i} - \\mathbf{j} - 12\\mathbf{k}.$", "So the angle bisector plane must have the form $12x - y - 12z + D = 0$ for some constant $D.$", "To find $D,$ I can use the given point $(-5,-1,-5)$ that lies on the plane.", "Substituting these values into the equation, I get $12(-5) - (-1) - 12(-5) + D = 0.$", "Simplifying, I get $-60 + 1 + 60 + D = 0.$", "So $D = -1.$", "Therefore, the equation of the angle bisector plane is $12x - y - 12z - 1 = 0.$", "# Answer\n\n12x - y - 12z - 1 = 0"], "pre_generated_answer": "12x - y - 12z - 1 = 0", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.004891231734490625}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "I know that the angle bisector plane must be parallel to the cross product of the normals of the given planes, since that vector is perpendicular to both planes and thus lies on the angle bisector.", "rating": -1, "flagged": null}, {"text": "I know that the equation of a plane that bisects the angle between two given planes is of the form $A(x - x_0) + B(y - y_0) + C(z - z_0) = k(\\alpha x + \\beta y + \\gamma z + \\delta),$ where $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ is any point on the bisecting plane, $k$ is a constant, and $\\alpha x + \\beta y + \\gamma z + \\delta$ is the equation of either of the given planes.", "rating": 0, "flagged": false}, {"text": "To solve this problem, I need to find a normal vector to the plane I'm looking for.", "rating": 0, "flagged": false}, {"text": "I need to find the equation of the plane that bisects the angle between two given planes and passes through a given point.", "rating": 0, "flagged": false}, {"text": "To find the equation of the plane that bisects the angle between the given planes, I need to find a normal vector that bisects the angle between the normal vectors of the given planes.", "rating": 0, "flagged": false}], "human_completion": null, "chosen_completion": null}], "total_time": 730650, "finish_reason": "found_error"}}
{"labeler": "340d89bc-f5b7-45e9-b272-909ba68ee363", "timestamp": "2023-02-05T01:52:43.648919", "generation": 9, "is_quality_control_question": false, "is_initial_screening_question": false, "question": {"problem": "The expression \\[(x+y+z)^{2006}+(x-y-z)^{2006}\\]is simplified by expanding it and combining like terms. How many terms are in the simplified expression?", "ground_truth_solution": "There is exactly one term in the simplified expression for every monomial of the form $x^ay^bz^c$, where $a,b$, and $c$ are non-negative integers, $a$ is even, and $a+b+c=2006$.  There are 1004 even values of $a$ with $0\\leq a\\leq 2006$.  For each such value, $b$ can assume any of the $2007-a$ integer values between 0 and $2006-a$, inclusive, and the value of $c$ is then uniquely determined as $2006-a-b$.  Thus the number of terms in the simplified expression is \\[\n(2007-0)+(2007-2)+\\cdots +(2007-2006)=2007+2005+\\cdots +1.\n\\]This is the sum of the first 1004 odd positive integers, which is $\n1004^2=\\boxed{1{,}008{,}016}.\n$\n\n\\[ OR \\]The given expression is equal to \\[\n\\sum \\frac{2006!}{a!b!c!}\n\\left(x^ay^bz^c + x^a(-y)^b(-z)^c \\right),\n\\]where the sum is taken over all non-negative integers $a,b,$ and $c$ with $a+b+c=2006$. Because the number of non-negative integer solutions of $a+b+c=k$ is $\\binom{k+2}{2}$, the sum is taken over $\\binom{2008}{2}$ terms, but those for which $b$ and $c$ have opposite parity have a sum of zero. If $b$ is odd and $c$ is even, then $a$ is odd, so $a=2A+1,b=2B+1,\n\\text{ and }c=2C$ for some non-negative integers $A,B,\\text{ and }C$. Therefore $2A+1+2B+1+2C=2006$, so $A+B+C=1002$. Because the last equation has $\\binom{1004}{2}$ non-negative integer solutions, there are $\\binom{1004}{2}$ terms for which $b$ is odd and $c$ is even. The number of terms for which $b$ is even and $c$ is odd is the same. Thus the number of terms in the simplified expression is \\[\\binom{2008}{2}-2\\binom{1004}{2} = 1004\\cdot 2007 - 1004\\cdot 1003 =\n1004^2 = \\boxed{1{,}008{,}016}.\\]", "ground_truth_answer": "1{,}008{,}016", "pre_generated_steps": ["I notice that the expression has the form \\[(a+b)^n+(a-b)^n\\]where \\[a=x, b=y+z, n=2006\\].", "I recall that the binomial theorem tells me how to expand \\[(a+b)^n\\]as a sum of terms of the form \\[\\binom{n}{k}a^{n-k}b^k\\]where \\[k=0,1,2,\\dots,n\\].", "I also recall that the binomial coefficients \\[\\binom{n}{k}\\]are symmetric, meaning that \\[\\binom{n}{k}=\\binom{n}{n-k}\\].", "I wonder what happens when I expand \\[(a-b)^n\\]using the same theorem.", "I see that I get a similar sum of terms, but with alternating signs: \\[(a-b)^n=\\sum_{k=0}^n (-1)^k \\binom{n}{k}a^{n-k}b^k\\].", "I realize that when I add \\[(a+b)^n+(a-b)^n\\], some of the terms will cancel out, and some will double up.", "I think about which terms will cancel out and which will double up.", "I observe that the terms with odd values of \\[k\\]will cancel out, because they have opposite signs in the two expansions.", "I infer that the terms with even values of \\[k\\]will double up, because they have the same sign in the two expansions.", "I deduce that the simplified expression will have only the terms with even values of \\[k\\], and each term will have a coefficient of \\[2\\binom{n}{k}\\].", "I count how many terms there are with even values of \\[k\\].", "I note that \\[k\\]can be any even integer between \\[0\\]and \\[n\\], inclusive.", "I estimate that there are about half as many even integers as there are integers between \\[0\\]and \\[n\\].", "I round up to the nearest integer, because \\[n\\]is even and \\[0\\]is included.", "I get that there are \\[\\frac{n}{2}+1\\]terms with even values of \\[k\\].", "I plug in \\[n=2006\\]and get that there are \\[1003+1=1004\\]terms in the simplified expression.", "# Answer\n\n1004"], "pre_generated_answer": "1004", "pre_generated_verifier_score": 0.004953411574789513}, "label": {"steps": [{"completions": [{"text": "I notice 